  "worked_solution": "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=-\\sqrt{2},$ we substitute $y=-\\sqrt{2}$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ 5(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ 5(2)-4{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ 10-4{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ -4{x}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{10}{4} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions. \nSo, the hyperbola does not intersect the horizontal line $y=-\\sqrt{2}.$",
